> The concern is only that a failing `__init__` leaves the encoder in a state that segfaults on attribute access. If re-init is not meant to be supported, a clear error on a second call would also close it.

I'm not sure I've ever seen or heard of a valid use case for "re-init", and I also don't know how you could ever end up with an object who's `__init__` has not been called. If you call `Encoder()` and its `__init__` fails, you don't get an `Encoder` instance. 

@sobolevn do you have an opinion on this? Are there any fringe cases I'm not aware of, aside from messing with `__new__`?
